        stat: 'Arctic foxes remain comfortable at −70°C — the most cold-adapted land mammal'
      },
      deep: {
        title: 'Deep Ocean',
        meta: 'Below 200m · perpetual darkness · up to 1,100 atmospheres',
        body: 'The deep ocean is the largest habitat on Earth (~90% of ocean volume). No sunlight penetrates below 200m — ecosystems depend on "marine snow" (sinking organic particles) or chemosynthesis at hydrothermal vents. Over 90% of deep-sea signalling uses bioluminescence. Animals at 4,000m experience 400 atmospheres; they use trimethylamine oxide to counteract pressure deformation of proteins. The first hydrothermal vent ecosystem was discovered in 1977 at the Galapagos Rift — entirely independent of solar energy, powered by chemosynthetic bacteria.',
        stat: 'Deep ocean = ~90% of ocean volume, most of it still unexplored'
      },
      reef: {
        title: 'Coral Reef',
        meta: '0.1% of ocean floor · ~25% of marine species',
        body: 'Coral reefs are highly productive despite nutrient-poor water because coral polyps efficiently recycle nutrients through their symbiotic zooxanthellae. A single reef may contain hundreds of fish species, thousands of invertebrate species, and dozens of coral species. Clownfish live in strict size-based dominance hierarchies within anemones; when the dominant female dies, the dominant male changes sex to replace her. Since 1950, approximately 50% of coral reef area has been lost to warming, ocean acidification, and pollution.',
        stat: '50% of coral reefs lost since 1950 — one of the fastest large-scale habitat losses recorded'
      },
      desert: {
        title: 'Desert',
        meta: 'Covers ~33% of Earth\'s land · less than 250mm rain per year',
        body: 'Deserts cover a third of Earth\'s land surface; the key challenge is water, not necessarily heat. The kangaroo rat never drinks liquid water — all moisture comes from metabolic oxidation of seeds; its kidneys concentrate urine to 14,000 mOsm (human maximum ~1,200). The thorny devil channels dew from its entire body surface via capillary grooves to its mouth. Fennec fox ears (15cm, ~40% of body size) are radiators to dump body heat. Desert scorpions can survive temperatures up to 45°C for hours and have been revived after being frozen solid.',
